-- Passage rg11-word-parts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a20ce9b9-aa4e-5b16-b827-5dbdd987f7a5', 'rg11-word-parts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Clock That Found the Sea$q$, $q$For centuries, sailors crossing the open ocean could measure their latitude from the height of the sun, but longitude remained a stubborn puzzle. To know how far east or west a ship had traveled, a navigator needed to compare local noon with the time at a fixed home port. That required a clock that kept accurate time for months while rolling on waves, shifting in temperature, and soaking in salt air.

Ordinary pendulum clocks were useless at sea, because every swell disturbed their swing. Many scholars theorized that the answer lay in the stars rather than in machinery, and they spent years compiling tables of lunar positions. Meanwhile, a few patient craftsmen pursued a different path: building a marine chronometer, a timekeeper so steady that it could carry home-port time across an entire ocean.

The early prototypes were heavy, temperamental, and enormously expensive. One designer spent decades refining his models, and a chronology of his work shows a steady shift from large brass frames to a device not much bigger than a pocket watch. Each version addressed a problem the last one had revealed, such as metal parts that expanded in tropical heat.

Trials at sea were demanding. Officers synchronized the chronometer with a trusted clock before departure, then checked it against known landmarks at the end of the voyage. A loss of even a few seconds a day could place a ship many miles off course, so the persistence of small errors mattered enormously.

Today, a historian who described an eighteenth-century captain checking a satellite screen would be guilty of an obvious anachronism. Yet the chronometer's legacy lingers in every modern navigation system, which still depends on one simple idea: knowing exactly what time it is somewhere else.$q$, 'draft')
