  ('94c701e7-e12c-5014-8349-6f1fcab144bc', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null, 'easy',
   $q$The Greek root chron means "time," and meter means "measure." What is a "chronometer" in the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"A chart that records the positions of the moon","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"context-only-guess"},{"text":"A tool that measures how hot the metal parts become","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-root-clues"},{"text":"An instrument that measures the depth of the ocean","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-first-root"},{"text":"An instrument that measures time with great precision","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$An instrument that measures time with great precision$q$, $q$Chron plus meter means "time measurer," and the passage calls the chronometer "a timekeeper so steady" that it carried home-port time across an ocean. The lunar tables are mentioned nearby, but they were the scholars' alternative, not the chronometer.$q$, 'draft', 'a20ce9b9-aa4e-5b16-b827-5dbdd987f7a5')
