   $q$The prefix ana- can mean "against" or "backward," and chron means "time." Why would describing an eighteenth-century captain checking a satellite screen be an "anachronism"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It places an object in a time period where it could not have existed.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It exaggerates how often captains checked their instruments.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It describes a navigation method that was too slow to be useful.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"context-only-guess"},{"text":"It confuses measuring longitude with measuring latitude.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-root-clues"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It places an object in a time period where it could not have existed.$q$, $q$An anachronism is something set against its proper time, like a satellite screen on an eighteenth-century ship. The passage does discuss latitude and longitude, but that mix-up has nothing to do with the root chron.$q$, 'draft', 'a20ce9b9-aa4e-5b16-b827-5dbdd987f7a5')
