-- Passage rg11-word-parts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dc3eaa1d-85e2-5b3d-979e-c317babdffc8', 'rg11-word-parts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Playing From Both Sides$q$, $q$Most people favor one hand from early childhood, and coaches in many sports spend years trying to strengthen the weaker side. A small number of athletes, however, are genuinely ambidextrous, able to throw, catch, or strike with either hand at nearly the same level of skill.

In table tennis, a player who can switch the paddle between hands is rare, but the rules allow it. More common are athletes who build partial versatility through practice. Basketball players drill left-handed layups for hours, and soccer players train their weaker foot until defenders can no longer predict which way they will cut.

Researchers who study hand preference note that it is not a simple switch. Some people write with the right hand but throw with the left, which makes the label "left-handed" surprisingly ambiguous. A survey that asks only "Which hand do you use?" may record the same person differently depending on which task that person pictures.

For young athletes, training both sides offers clear benefits. It improves coordination, reduces the strain of repeating one motion thousands of times, and makes a player harder to defend. Still, some coaches feel ambivalent about pushing the idea too far. They admire the flexibility it builds, yet they worry that splitting practice time could keep a player from mastering a strong side first.

The evidence so far suggests a balanced approach. Players who develop real competence with the weaker hand, without abandoning the strength of the dominant one, tend to gain the most. In sports that reward surprise, being difficult to predict is often worth as much as raw power.$q$, 'draft')
