-- Passage rg11-word-parts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2b8d33eb-bae9-53db-a112-7bad5ec265b4', 'rg11-word-parts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Back Room$q$, $q$Hana Sato spent her summer as an intern at the Millbrook Historical Museum, a converted brick bank on the town square. On her first morning, the curator, Dr. Folasade Adeyemi, led her to a dusty back room labeled Anthropology. "This is where we keep objects that tell us how people in this valley lived," Dr. Adeyemi said. "Tools, clothing, toys, letters. Anything human."

The room was being cleaned out for a new exhibit funded by a local philanthropist, a retired pharmacist named Mr. Castellanos who had donated money to the museum, the library, and the town clinic for thirty years. He had requested a display about childhood in the valley a century ago.

Hana's job was to catalog the toys. Most were simple: wooden tops, marbles, a cloth ball. The most popular item, she suspected, would be a set of anthropomorphic animal puppets, foxes and rabbits dressed in tiny waistcoats and bonnets, each with a painted human smile.

Among the boxes she also found a bundle of letters written by a schoolteacher named Clara Whitfield. In one letter, Clara described a neighbor as a misanthrope who refused to attend town picnics and scowled at every child who passed his gate. In a later letter, though, Clara admitted she had misjudged him. He had quietly repaired the school's broken desks every winter without telling anyone.

Hana showed the letters to Dr. Adeyemi, who read them carefully. "This is exactly why we study objects and documents together," the curator said. "A puppet shows us what children played with. A letter shows us how people saw one another, and how those views could change."

The letters went into the exhibit beside the puppets, under a small card that Hana wrote herself.$q$, 'draft')
