-- Passage rg11-word-parts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('059c876a-b458-587c-a6b4-bdc4eeabf8a8', 'rg11-word-parts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Rock in the Shoebox$q$, $q$The tip arrived in the student newspaper's inbox on a Monday: a sophomore named Elias Brandt claimed he had found a meteorite in the creek behind the football field. The message included a blurry photo of a dark, pitted rock.

Diego Fuentes, the paper's science reporter, was incredulous. "Do you know how rare that would be?" he asked the editor, Amara Nwosu. "Most rocks people call meteorites turn out to be leftover slag from old factories."

Amara agreed that the claim needed checking before the paper gave it any credence. "If we print it and it's wrong, we lose credibility with every reader," she said. "Find someone who actually knows."

Diego called the geology department at the community college and reached Dr. Ines Marchetti, who listed her credentials before he even asked: a doctorate in planetary science and fifteen years identifying samples for a regional museum. She agreed to examine the rock that afternoon.

Elias brought it in a shoebox. Dr. Marchetti tested it with a magnet, sanded a small corner, and studied the metal flecks under a lens. Finally she smiled. "This is the real thing," she said. "An iron meteorite, probably thousands of years old."

Diego's story ran the following week with photographs of the test. A few readers still posted doubtful comments, and one tried to discredit the find by suggesting the rock had been bought online. But Dr. Marchetti's statement, printed in full, answered every objection.

Amara pinned the article to the newsroom wall. "Being skeptical wasn't the opposite of being fair," she told Diego. "It's what made people believe us."$q$, 'draft')
