-- Passage rg11-word-parts-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b645b244-bb93-53d6-81a6-6f139575506a', 'rg11-word-parts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Measuring the Shaking Ground$q$, $q$Earthquakes are far more common than most people realize. Sensitive instruments record thousands of small tremors around the world every day, so many that seismic activity is nearly omnipresent beneath our feet, even when no one feels a thing.

Scientists describe each earthquake by its magnitude, a number that reflects how much energy was released at the source. The scale is logarithmic, which means each whole number represents roughly thirty times more energy than the one below it. A magnitude 6 earthquake is not slightly stronger than a magnitude 5; it is enormously stronger.

Magnitude is different from intensity, which describes how strongly the shaking is felt at a particular place. A single earthquake has one magnitude but many intensities, because the shaking weakens with distance and changes with the ground underneath. Loose, wet soil can magnify the motion, making a moderate quake feel severe in one neighborhood while a rocky hillside nearby barely trembles.

To compare earthquakes recorded on different continents, seismologists had to standardize their methods. Stations now use similar instruments and shared formulas, so a measurement taken in one country can be checked against readings from dozens of others.

Modern sensors are also omnidirectional, detecting ground motion whether it arrives from the north, the south, or directly below. When several stations record the same event, computers combine the readings to locate its source within minutes. That speed allows warnings to reach nearby cities seconds before the strongest waves arrive, enough time for trains to slow and for workers to step away from heavy machinery.$q$, 'draft')
