   $q$The Latin root magn means "great," and the suffix -tude names a state or quality. What does an earthquake's "magnitude" describe?$q$, $q$[{"text":"How long the shaking lasted at one location","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-root-clues"},{"text":"How far away from a city the earthquake struck","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"context-only-guess"},{"text":"How great the energy released at its source was","is_correct":true},{"text":"How much damage it caused in one neighborhood","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-related-term"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$How great the energy released at its source was$q$, $q$Magnitude names greatness of size, and the passage says it "reflects how much energy was released at the source." Damage in one neighborhood relates to intensity, which the passage explicitly separates from magnitude.$q$, 'draft', 'b645b244-bb93-53d6-81a6-6f139575506a')
