insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5a92fa28-9abe-56ef-82f3-14efcea084e7', 'rg11-word-parts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Board Seven$q$, $q$Kofi Mensah had grown up in a household where three languages drifted through every meal, and by sixteen he was something of a polyglot, comfortable in English, French, and Twi, with a growing command of Spanish. So when the city hosted an international youth chess tournament, the organizers asked him to volunteer as an interpreter.

The job required him to be more versatile than he expected. In a single morning he translated the rules for a player from Senegal, helped a coach from Peru find the right bus, and explained a seating chart to a family from Quebec. The players came from diverse backgrounds, but the games themselves were played in near silence.

The trouble began in the fourth round. A digital clock on board seven began to malfunction, adding minutes to one player's time while the other player's display froze. Neither player noticed until the arbiter, Ms. Petrova, saw the numbers jump.

The player from Peru, Rafael Quispe, looked worried. He spoke little English and feared he would be blamed. Kofi knelt beside him and translated as Ms. Petrova examined the clock. "No one here is culpable," she said firmly. "This is a battery fault, not anyone's doing." She reset both times based on the moves recorded on the score sheets.

Afterward Rafael thanked Kofi in Spanish, then tried the words again in English. Kofi laughed and answered in both.

That evening his aunt asked whether all his language study had paid off. Kofi thought about it. "Before today it was mostly theoretical," he said. "I knew I could translate. Today I actually did it, and it mattered."$q$, 'draft')
